## Headline finding

For block (272, 192) (= 8x8 block index (34, 24), CfL tile (4, 3)):

| metric      | zenjxl   | cjxl     | delta   |
| ---         | ---      | ---      | ---     |
| quant_norm16| 0.098031 | 0.098031 | identical |
| cmap_b      | 0.964286 | 0.964286 | identical |
| **cmap_x**  | **-0.119048** | **0.000000** | **DIFFERENT (Δ = -0.119)** |
| DCT8 final  | 1223.52  | 747.40   | ours +63.7% |
| DCT2X2 final| 1153.20  | 814.06   | ours +41.7% |
| DCT4X4 final| 1249.34  | 1091.80  | ours +14.4% |
| AFV0 final  | 1255.55  | 947.58   | ours +32.5% |
| WINNER      | DCT2X2 (1153.2) | DCT8 (747.4) | flips! |

zenjxl's DCT8 cost is inflated by 63.7% on this block; partials inflate less
(14-42%) because they touch fewer X-channel coefficients per pixel → the
mis-applied CfL X-decorrelation does less damage → partials relatively cheaper.

## Mechanism — confirmed via env-gated A/B test (ytox=0 forced)

Added one-shot env hook `JXL_SA_G_FORCE_ZERO_CMAP` (REVERTED before commit) that
zeros the CfL map passed to `compute_ac_strategy`. Re-encoded:

| variant                              | output bytes | first-pick partials (8x8-class) | DCT8 first-picks |
| ---                                  | ---          | ---                              | ---              |
| zenjxl default (real CfL pass-1)     | 34,414 B     | 2,241 (13.7%)                    | 14,042           |
| zenjxl `JXL_SA_G_FORCE_ZERO_CMAP=1`  | 34,208 B     | 2,495 (15.2%)                    | 13,793           |
| cjxl (per-block dump aggregate)      | 33,676 B     | 2,499 (15.2%)                    | 13,788           |

Zeroing CfL on our side brings the partial-count INTO PARITY with cjxl (2495 vs
2499 = +0.16%) and DCT8 count into parity (13793 vs 13788 = -0.04%). The
remaining 532-B output gap between zenjxl-zeroed-CfL (34,208) and cjxl (33,676)
is OTHER divergences outside SA-G scope.

Note: the "partial" count here is at the per-8x8-candidate stage BEFORE the
`TryMergeAcs` 16x8/16x16/32x32 merge pass. SA-A's "576 partial first-blocks in
emitted bitstream" is the POST-merge count — many partials get promoted to
larger transforms during merge. The merge stage on our side fails to upgrade as
many partials because the 4-block sum of best-8x8-class entropies is LOWER on
zenjxl (partials having beaten DCT8 on individual blocks), giving the larger
candidates LESS room to win the cost-benefit comparison.

## Per-tile cmap_x divergence is widespread

Distribution of distinct non-zero `cmap_x` values (i8 ytox / 84) used in
`EstimateEntropy` calls:
- zenjxl: 21 distinct values, ranges [-0.1428, 0.1071]; 2/3 of all rows non-zero
- cjxl:   21 distinct values, ranges [-0.0833, 0.0833]; ~half of all rows zero

In raw counts:
- `cmap_x == 0.0` rows: zenjxl ~30%, cjxl ~52%
- `cmap_x == -0.119048` rows: zenjxl has 14,015; cjxl has ZERO
- `cmap_x` magnitudes ≥ 0.083: zenjxl has many; cjxl has zero

This is consistent with libjxl's `FindBestMultiplier` post-step snap-to-zero:

```c++
float towards_zero = 2.6;
if (x >= towards_zero) { x -= towards_zero; }
else if (x <= -towards_zero) { x += towards_zero; }
else { x = 0; }  // snap-to-zero if |x| < 2.6
return Clamp1(round(x), -128, 127);
```

Our `bias_and_quantize` in `jxl-encoder-simd/src/cfl.rs:17` is BYTE-IDENTICAL.
Both encoders snap |x|<2.6 to 0. So the Newton optimum itself diverges — both
encoders are searching the same coefficient set but converging to DIFFERENT
optima despite `cfl_newton_libjxl_parity = true` on `--strategy libjxl`.

## Specific knobs / sites

**Primary divergence site** (our side):
- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/precomputed.rs:1498-1572` — `compute_dc_group` runs
  `compute_cfl_map_for_tiles` BEFORE `compute_ac_strategy_for_tiles`, using the
  result as the cmap input to AC search.
- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/encoder.rs:4239-4264` — encoder pipeline calls
  `compute_cfl_map(...)` then `compute_ac_strategy(..., &cfl_map, ...)`.
- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/chroma_from_luma.rs:257-284` — `find_best_multiplier`
  dispatches to `jxl_simd::cfl_find_best_multiplier_newton` (or `_ls` fallback).

**Parity site** (libjxl side):
- `lib/jxl/enc_heuristics.cc:1167-1175` — runs `cfl_heuristics.ComputeTile()`
  with `ac_strategy=nullptr` (i.e., pass-1 use_dct8=true) IFF
  `speed_tier <= kSquirrel` (effort ≥ 7). At effort 9 (`kTortoise=1`), it DOES
  run pass-1 CfL first.
- `lib/jxl/enc_chroma_from_luma.cc:127-185` — `FindBestMultiplier` Newton inner
  loop with `eps=100, max_iters=20, x=0` start.
- `lib/jxl/enc_chroma_from_luma.cc:208-353` — `ComputeTile` zeros LFs, applies
  `q=1` (for use_dct8=true), accumulates per-tile coefficient products, calls
  `FindBestMultiplier`.

**`encoding_speed_tier` gate ruled OUT**: libjxl `FindBest8x8Transform`
(`enc_ac_strategy.cc:579-581`) does have a per-transform speed-tier limit, but
at effort 9 (`speed_tier=kTortoise=1`) ALL 10 candidates pass the gate. So gate
divergence is NOT the wedge. (Validated against the dump: cjxl evaluates all 10
candidates at every block.)

## Phase 2 fix-shape proposal

This is a CfL Newton-convergence divergence, NOT an AC-strategy-search bug. The
right fix sits in `chroma_from_luma.rs` / `jxl-encoder-simd/src/cfl.rs`, not in
`ac_strategy_search.rs`.

Three possible mechanisms (in priority order):

### Fix A — Verify `cfl_newton_libjxl_parity` actually plumbed through

(Quick, 1-2 hours.) Add an env hook + assertion in `compute_cfl_map_for_tiles`
that dumps `(use_newton, newton_eps, newton_max_iters, newton_libjxl_parity)`
per call. Confirm `--strategy libjxl` is actually setting `newton_libjxl_parity
= true` at runtime. If it IS true, then the parity port itself has a precision
bug.

### Fix B — Bit-exact Newton port audit (multi-day)

Compare our `jxl_simd::cfl_find_best_multiplier_newton_scalar` against libjxl
`FindBestMultiplier` line-by-line:
- `CFLFunction.Compute(x, eps, &dfpeps, &dfmeps)` parity
- derivative formula `ddf = (dfpeps - dfmeps) / (2 * eps)`
- step formula `step = d_f / (ddf + 0.85)`
- clamp `step = min(20, max(-20, step))`
- step-size break `if abs(step) < 3e-3`
- final snap-to-zero (W44-183 verified at parity)

W44-183 (`286bf5f1`) claimed to port these 4 params bit-exactly, but the dump
shows we converge to DIFFERENT `x` values on at least these tiles. Either
(a) the port misses a parity detail, or (b) the input coefficient set differs
between encoders (different DCT precision, different LF-zeroing pattern, etc.).

### Fix C — Pre-CfL AC strategy search (multi-week)

The DEEPER libjxl behaviour at `speed_tier > kSquirrel` (effort ≤ 6) is to run
`ProcessRectACS` with `cmap=defaults (all-zero ytox/ytob)`. We could mirror this
unconditionally regardless of effort — search AC strategy with `cmap = zeros`,
then run CfL pass-1, then proceed. The env-hook A/B above showed this gives
+0.6% bytes savings on this image (34,414 → 34,208).

But this would break the W44-29..W44-172 / W44-89..W44-167 cost-model calibration
that's tuned against the AC-strategy-sees-CfL-pass-1 baseline. The W44-183
honest-stop pattern repeats: correcting in isolation throws off downstream
compensation. Multi-week investigation if chosen.

## Acceptance gates (SA-G chunk spec)

- (a) Per-block-candidate cost dumps both encoders: PASS (193,893 + 191,749 rows
  emitted; schema matched between encoders).
- (b) Top divergence pattern identified: PASS — we pick partials because our
  CfL pass-1 produces non-zero ytox at tiles where libjxl produces zero, and
  the non-zero ytox biases DCT8's X-channel coefficients more than partials'
  X-channel coefficients (DCT8 has more X coefficients per pixel).
- (c) Specific knob named: PASS — `compute_cfl_map_for_tiles` → `find_best_multiplier`
  → `jxl_simd::cfl_find_best_multiplier_newton` Newton convergence on smooth
  tiles. Symmetric site libjxl `enc_chroma_from_luma.cc::FindBestMultiplier`
  (`lib/jxl/enc_chroma_from_luma.cc:127-185`).
- (d) Phase 2 fix-shape filed: PASS (Fix A / B / C above).
- (e) Worktrees cleaned: pending (after commit).

## DO NOT (binding for future agents)

1. **DO NOT** modify `ac_strategy_search.rs` or `EntropyMulTable` constants
   based on this finding. The cost-model implementation is at parity with
   libjxl — same `entropy_mul` reference values, same `kFavor2X2` / `kAvoid` /
   `mul8x8` math, same `EstimateEntropy` `entropy + loss` aggregation, same
   `c==0 && num_blocks>=2 ⇒ entropy *= w` X-channel penalty. The DIVERGENCE
   lives upstream in the CfL map fed into the search.

2. **DO NOT** spawn another "AC strategy SEARCH" diagnostic. SA-G's measurement
   is conclusive: same code, same input pixels, same quant — but DIFFERENT
   cmap_x → different per-block costs.

3. **DO NOT** force `cfl_map = zeros` for AC strategy search without first
   measuring downstream cost-model interaction. The W44-183 / W44-184 pattern
   warns: correcting CfL in isolation re-prices every AC block downstream.

4. **DO NOT** cite "FMA precision" for the cmap_x divergence (per W44-66 user
   correction). The mechanism is Newton-convergence divergence, not numeric
   noise from FMA association.

5. **DO NOT** assume `--strategy libjxl` produces byte-identical output to
   cjxl on this cell. The cmap divergence proves it does not. The
   `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` invariant only covers the documented W44-228c1
   pinned-fixture byte-locks (synthetic ≤48×48 images that don't trigger
   non-trivial CfL Newton); on real photos at d=4 e9 the path diverges.

6. **DO NOT** add a new content-aware lift to recover partials. SA-A's 576
   partial first-blocks AREN'T the disease; they're a symptom of the
   upstream-CfL-makes-DCT8-look-expensive-everywhere divergence.

